# The local secrets enclave (docs/data-feeds.md §12).
#
# Copy this file to `secrets.local.env` (git-ignored) and replace <vault> with
# the name of your own 1Password vault. Nothing here is a secret: every value
# is either a vault name or an `op://` *reference*, and the values themselves
# stay in 1Password. `make collectors-up` resolves the references through
# `op run` and hands the results to the collector containers, which never reach
# a secret store themselves.
#
# DO NOT `source` this file. It holds references, not credentials — sourcing it
# would put the reference string itself into OANDA_API_KEY, where it is a
# perfectly non-empty value that is not a key. The provider refuses an `op://`
# value outright rather than handing it to a venue.
#
# 1Password is the local mock of AWS Secrets Manager: an item per provider, a
# named field per credential, so a canonical `<provider>/<secret>` name is
# already a valid reference tail and the same name is a valid Secrets Manager
# id under the `dropset/` prefix. A second credential from a provider already
# here is a new field on that item; one an app resolves also needs its
# SECRET_NAME constant and call site in code.

# The vault to resolve against, read by `op run` and by the Makefile. Note the
# Rust provider reads this from its own *environment* and never parses this
# file, so a collector run straight from the host names the vault on the
# command line instead:
#
#   DROPSET_OP_VAULT=<vault> cargo run --bin market-data-oanda
DROPSET_OP_VAULT=<vault>

# UNCOMMENT AND EDIT only if `op` is signed in to more than one account, where
# a bare read cannot disambiguate and fails. It ships commented out because the
# Makefile expands this value into a shell command line: left as the literal
# placeholder below, `<account>.1password.com` would be parsed by sh as a pair
# of redirects and fail with an error naming no part of this file.
#
# Read twice, and it has to be: the Rust provider takes it from the process
# environment, while the Makefile `include`s this file to pass it to `op run`
# as `--account`. `op run` injects an env file into the *child*, so a value
# only written here would never reach `op` itself.
#DROPSET_OP_ACCOUNT=<account>.1password.com

# The free-tier FX roster (docs/data-feeds.md §9). Each variable name is
# derived from the collector's canonical secret name — do not rename one
# without renaming the other.
#
# Keep all three. `make collectors-up` warns loudly when this file is absent
# or cannot be resolved, but it CANNOT see a file that resolves while missing
# one line: `op run` leaves that variable to the ambient environment, compose
# passes it as empty, and that one collector starts, names the variable it
# wanted, and dies while the others record normally.
OANDA_API_KEY=op://<vault>/oanda/api-key
TWELVEDATA_API_KEY=op://<vault>/twelvedata/api-key
ALPHAVANTAGE_API_KEY=op://<vault>/alphavantage/api-key

# Pyth is the fourth KEYED collector and is deliberately NOT in the list
# above.
# Hermes went from keyless to keyed on 2026-08-26, and Pyth sells no usable
# free tier: theirs is view-only in a web terminal with no API permissions,
# the self-serve key is a time-boxed Pro trial, and the plan covering FX —
# the only reason this collector exists — is $2,500/month (docs/data-feeds.md
# §9). So the expected state is that nobody running this stack holds a key.
#
# UNCOMMENT ONLY IF YOU ACTUALLY HOLD ONE, for the same reason the account
# line above ships commented out: a reference to a vault item that does not
# exist is worse than an absent line. An absent line falls through to the
# ambient environment, which the paragraph above describes; a *present but
# unresolvable* one is a different `op run` failure, and `op run` wraps the
# bring-up of all three collectors above, so it can take OANDA, Twelve Data
# and Alpha Vantage down with it.
#
# The pyth collector is not started by `make collectors-up` either way — it
# sits behind the compose `pyth` profile.
#PYTH_API_KEY=op://<vault>/pyth/api-key
